<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Deogarh Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1590 CE – 1743 CE (approx.) · capital Deogarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Gond kingdom of the Satpura hills whose rulers founded Nagpur and were eventually displaced by the Bhonsle Marathas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond</a> kingdom of Deogarh emerged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhindwara-district">Chhindwara</a> region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satpura-range">Satpura</a> hills in the late 16th century. Its first well-attested ruler, Jatba, is mentioned in Mughal sources and acknowledged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar's</a> suzerainty, as recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ain-i-akbari">Ain-i-Akbari</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Deogarh ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond</a> farming and forest communities and grew under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakht-buland-shah">Bakht Buland Shah</a>, who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim settlers to clear land. He founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> around 1702, which became the capital under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-sultan">Chand Sultan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-sultan">Chand Sultan's</a> death in 1739 a succession dispute invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raghuji-i-bhonsle">Raghoji Bhonsle</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> to intervene, and by 1743 he had taken effective control, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> the seat of the Bhonsle Maratha state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond</a> family survived as pensioners.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/deogarh-kingdom.jpg" alt="Deogarh Kingdom" width="320" height="373"><figcaption>Image: Pls I dank memer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Approximate_extent_of_the_Gond_kingdom_of_Deogarh.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1590 CE – 1743 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Deogarh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jatba, Bakht Buland Shah, Chand Sultan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Gond religion, Islam, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gondi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gonds of Deogarh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1666&amp;focus=deogarh-kingdom" aria-label="Deogarh Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6759 1275 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Deogarh Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7079 1531L7018 1385M7079 1531L7126 1652"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7126 1652h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7079 1531h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7079 1531h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Deogarh. <a href="/atlas?year=1666&amp;focus=deogarh-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
